---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import AdSlot from '../components/AdSlot.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site.ts';
---

<Base
  title="Editorial team"
  description="Who writes the guides, who reviews them, and what the review actually checks before a page is published."
  path="/editorial-team/"
>
  <div class="container">
    <div class="page-head">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
        <span>Editorial team</span>
      </nav>
      <h1>Editorial team</h1>
      <p class="page-head__lede">
        A small site publishes a small number of pages, and every one of them
        carries a name against it. This page says who does what and what the
        review checks.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid--2" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-12);">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--text-muted);">Author</p>
        <h2 class="card__title" style="margin-top: var(--space-2);">
          {SITE.author.name}
        </h2>
        <p class="card__body">{SITE.author.bio}</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="eyebrow" style="color: var(--text-muted);">Reviewer</p>
        <h2 class="card__title" style="margin-top: var(--space-2);">
          {SITE.reviewer.name}
        </h2>
        <p class="card__body">{SITE.reviewer.bio}</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>What the review checks</h2>
      <p>
        Before a guide is published, the reviewer works through the same list
        every time:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The arithmetic.</strong> Every formula is checked against
          the source it comes from, and the worked example in the text is
          recalculated from scratch. Where a figure appears in the code, the
          two are compared.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The units.</strong> Percentages against decimals, months
          against years, dollars against thousands. Most mistakes in a
          financial document are a unit error.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The edge cases.</strong> A zero rate, a zero term, a negative
          value. Each is checked to produce a sensible answer rather than an
          error message.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The wording.</strong> The page must not tell the reader what
          to do. It explains what a number means and what it depends on, and
          stops there. A guide that recommends an action gets rewritten.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The sources.</strong> Each link is opened, checked for the
          claim it is attached to, and dated.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How drafting tools are used</h2>
      <p>
        Drafting tools help with the prose. They do not decide what a page
        says. Every figure, every formula and every claim is written or
        checked by a person, and no page goes live without the review above.
        A tool that produced a plausible number is exactly the kind of thing
        this process exists to catch.
      </p>

      <h2>Review dates</h2>
      <p>
        Every guide carries the date it was last reviewed. A guide whose
        content depends on outside data is reviewed at least twice a year; a
        guide that explains arithmetic is reviewed when the formula behind it
        changes, and in any case once a year. When a page is reviewed the
        date moves, whether or not the text changed, so the date tells you
        when a person last read the page.
      </p>

      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        Corrections are made in the open. A page that has been corrected keeps
        its review date updated and the correction is described in the
        <a href="/contact/">contact</a> thread it came from. A guide with a
        wrong figure is a defect in the review, not in the writing, and the
        review process is what changes.
      </p>
    </div>

    <AdSlot placement="footer" />
  </div>
</Base>
